 **Notes to next run (v3).** Unchanged from the eighth run, plus: the bot behaviours that
 parse system lines ignore TOO_FAST already; keep it that way in `gameplay_gather_v3.gd`.
+
+## 2026-11-23 (tenth run, 00:00 Chicago): protocol v3, real gathering
+
+**What I built.** The bump the last three runs were waiting for (first run of the owner's
+new day; workstreams.md also dropped the one-bump-a-day rule this morning).
+- `game/shared/net_play.gd`, created by `net.gd` as the child `Net/Play` in `_init` (not
+  `_ready`, so `net.play` exists before any role connects). It carries `c2s_gather_start`,
+  `c2s_gather_cancel`, `c2s_craft`, `s2c_gather_begin/end/result`, `s2c_inventory`,
+  `s2c_nodes`, `s2c_node_state`, `s2c_craft_result`, and each one re-emits a signal on Net,
+  so callers keep connecting to `net.<signal>`. `Net`'s own RPC list is unchanged apart
+  from the argument below, so v1/v2 clients still get "out of date" (protocol test passes
+  for v1, v2, 2 and 4 over websocket and ENet).
+- `ack_seq`: a third argument on `s2c_snapshot`, the receiver's last applied input seq (-1
+  before any). Snapshots were already sent per peer, so it costs one int. I kept the
+  `snapshot(tick, entries)` signal as it was and added `input_acked(tick, ack_seq)`, emitted
+  just before it, so client.gd and every bot behaviour that connects to `snapshot` keeps
+  working untouched. Changing an existing RPC's arguments is safe here: snapshots only go
+  to logged-in peers, and only current clients get past login.
+- Server: `_on_gather_start` -> `gather_request`, `_on_gather_cancel` -> `gather_cancel(id,
+  "stopped")`, `_on_craft` -> `craft_request`. Logged-out peers are ignored. Gather start
+  and craft are each taken at most once per 0.1 s (`PLAY_REQUEST_GAP`), because each one
+  makes the server answer and a flooding client shouldn't make it spam back.
+- `/gather` is gone from `HELP` (still works, for bots and the current client's press-E).
+- `PROTOCOL_VERSION` 3.
+
+**Tests.** `tests/unit/test_gameplay_protocol_v3.gd` (10 tests): Play child exists from the
+start and Net has none of its RPCs; every message a full scenario sends has an RPC with the
+same argument count (this catches a server/net mismatch that `_send` would otherwise only
+log once); RPCs re-emit; ack before snapshot; per-receiver ack in `_tick`; start, cancel,
+logged-out and flood paths. Updated the v3-hooks routing test and the /help test. 288 unit
+tests pass with Postgres. Bot test 8 x 180 s websocket passed; new
+`gameplay_gather_v3.gd` (replaces `gameplay_gather_channel.gd`, which used `/channel`):
+checks `s2c_inventory` and `s2c_nodes` on every login, then walk-away ("moved"), explicit
+cancel ("stopped") and hold to "done" with result and pack, all through the RPCs. Protocol
+test (websocket and ENet) and client smoke passed.
+
+**What didn't fit / known gaps.**
+- "Two bots share one node" is counted (`shared_node`), not asserted: 8 bots spread over
+  40 m rarely pick the same node in the same second. The contested case is in the unit tests.
+- Players see no difference until client switches press-E and the craft window to the RPCs
+  (request in client's backlog). The server still sends the system lines, so the switch can
+  land whenever.
+- Frozen copies for v4: `net_v3.gd` will preload `res://shared/net_play.gd`, which will
+  change. Freeze a `net_play_v3.gd` with it (follow-up in the backlog).
+
+**Notes to next run.** Next up: skills data and XP server side (Milestone 1 part 1, no
+bump), unless client has switched to `c2s_gather_start`, in which case do the chat gate
+first. Write the "which MMO does skills best" devlog lines before building it.
